 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Replacement
+
+The cache is a 2Q cache. It keeps repeatedly consulted comparison sheets resident while a long sequential screening streams one-use sheets past them.
+
+Every cached sheet sits on one of two byte-bounded queues, and recently evicted first-use keys sit on a key-only ghost queue:
+
+| Queue | Holds | Entry | Order |
+|-------|-------|-------|-------|
+| A1in | sheets seen once | JPEG bytes | FIFO |
+| A1out | keys of sheets recently evicted from A1in | key only, no JPEG bytes | FIFO |
+| Am | sheets seen at least twice | JPEG bytes | LRU |
+
+- On a miss, the completed sheet enters the back of A1in.
+- On a hit for a sheet on A1in, promote the sheet to the most-recent end of Am.
+- On a hit for a sheet on Am, move it to the most-recent end of Am.
+- On a miss whose key is on A1out (a ghost hit), admit the completed sheet directly to the most-recent end of Am and drop the ghost key.
+- When A1in exceeds its byte share, evict its oldest sheet and record its key at the back of A1out.
+- When A1out exceeds its entry bound, drop its oldest key.
+- When admitting a sheet would push total cached JPEG data above 12 GiB, evict from A1in first, then from the least-recent end of Am, until the sheet fits.
+
+A one-use sheet in a sequential screening therefore cycles through A1in and A1out without displacing a reused comparison sheet on Am. Ghost keys are metadata and do not count toward the 12 GiB JPEG bound.
+
+The A1in/Am byte split and the A1out entry bound are preview-worker configuration. Starting values are 3 GiB for A1in, 9 GiB for Am, and an A1out bound equal to the number of A1in entries the byte share can hold; these are expected to be tuned against observed workloads.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: A1in (FIFO, first-use), A1out (key-only ghost), Am (LRU, reused) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps reused comparison sheets out of the path of a long screening scan: one-use sheets stay on A1in/A1out while Am retains reuse. LRU has the lowest bookkeeping and adapts quickly to project switches, but a sequential screening evicts useful comparison sheets. Aged frequency retains repeatedly popular clips but adds counter maintenance and aging and can keep yesterday's favorites too long. 2Q costs two queues plus a split to tune; all of that bookkeeping stays inside the preview worker and does not change the external interface, render concurrency, or the disk budget. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
